# Expectation record for 05_pointers/001_arithmetic_on_arrays.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 001_arithmetic_on_arrays
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Pointer increment, decrement and indexing scaled by element size, folded and volatile-runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every pointer formed stays within its array, and every index used is inside bounds:
i_arr and s_arr have 8 elements and are indexed 0..7, d_arr and p_arr have 4 and are
indexed 0..3. Pointers are advanced and retreated only across positions that exist, and
each is dereferenced only while it designates a real element. The byte-distance
computations subtract two pointers to elements of one array object, which is defined,
then scale that element count by the element size. No character view of a distinct
element subobject is subtracted, because such a difference would not be defined.
No signed integer overflow, no shift out of range, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. The only external symbol is printf; no header is included,
no file, socket, environment variable, clock or random source is touched, and every input
is a literal in this source file.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Element-size scaling is printed as a byte distance between adjacent elements of the same
array. sizeof(short), sizeof(int), sizeof(double) and sizeof(struct pair {int; int;}) are
2, 4, 8 and 8 on all four supported targets, so those distances are target independent.
No address or pointer value is printed. Pointer facts appear only as differences,
comparisons and alignment residues, and every pointer difference is cast to long long
before printing, so the printed text is identical whether ptrdiff_t is 32 or 64 bits.
Plain char is never printed in a signedness-dependent way. The only conversion
specifiers used anywhere in this program are %d, %lld and fixed-precision %.3f, all of
which are target-width independent.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
folded_index=10 20 50 80
folded_offset=60
folded_preinc=70
folded_postinc=80
folded_predec=70
folded_postdec=60
folded_plusassign=80
folded_minusassign=40
folded_walked_index=3
folded_short=1 4 8
folded_double=0.500 3.500
folded_struct=1 2 7 8
folded_scale_short=2
folded_scale_int=4
folded_scale_double=8
folded_scale_struct=8
folded_scale_int_span=16
runtime_index=40
runtime_index_plus=40
runtime_index_commuted=40
runtime_plusassign=40
runtime_minusassign=20
runtime_walked_index=1
runtime_short=6
runtime_double=2.500
runtime_struct=7 8
runtime_scale_int=4
runtime_scale_double=8
runtime_scan_total=360
END
